channels: Channels

chan T is gp::chan<T>, a shared reference to the channel: copies, and the directional views
chan<- T and <-chan T, are the same C++ type and share one channel. make(chan T, n) is
gp::make_chan<T>(n). A send is gp::send(ch, v), a receive gp::recv(ch), or gp::recv2(ch) for
v, ok := <-ch. Sending blocks until a receiver is ready or the buffer has room; sending on a
closed channel panics; a nil channel blocks forever. After close, a receive drains the
buffer and then gives the zero value, with ok false.

for v := range ch receives until the channel is closed (gp::range_chan). The runtime's
channel locks and parks the thread; there is no spinning.

Specification: docs/spec/cpp-mapping.md#goroutines-and-channels
See also: gocpp explain select, gocpp explain goroutines, gocpp explain range
Also known as: channel, chan, send, receive

Example (channels.gp):

    package main

    import "fmt"

    func main() {
    	ch := make(chan int, 2)
    	go func() {
    		for i := 0; i < 3; i++ {
    			ch <- i
    		}
    		close(ch)
    	}()
    	for v := range ch {
    		fmt.Println(v)
    	}
    	v, ok := <-ch
    	fmt.Println(v, ok)
    }

The C++ that gocpp emit -annotate writes for it:

    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #pragma once
    #include <gocpp/rt.hpp>

    namespace gpkg_main {
    void main();

    } // namespace gpkg_main
    //// gpkg/command-line-arguments/channels.gp.cpp
    // gpkg/command-line-arguments/channels.gp.cpp: generated by gocpp, do not edit
    #include "gpkg/command-line-arguments/pkg.hpp"

    namespace gpkg_main {

    // ---- ./channels.gp:5: func main() {
    void main() {

    // ---- ./channels.gp:6: ch := make(chan int, 2)
    // gp[channels] col 8: make(chan): allocates the channel; a gp::chan is a shared reference, so
    //     copies and directional views share one channel
        gp::chan<int64_t> ch = gp::make_chan<int64_t>(2);

    // ---- ./channels.gp:7: go func() {
    // gp[goroutines] col 2: go: the function and its arguments are evaluated now, in this goroutine;
    //     the call runs on a new goroutine (an OS thread, ADR-007/026): a func literal: its lambda is
    //     passed to gp::go_call, so the stack trace names it func1, func2...
    // gp[closures] col 5: func literal #1 is a C++ lambda (the stack trace names it func1); what it
    //     captures is copied into the lambda (a box is shared), and a lambda that is used as a func
    //     value is wrapped in gp::func, whose copies share the captured state
        gp::go_call([ch]<int = 1>() mutable -> void {

    // ---- ./channels.gp:8: for i := 0; i < 3; i++ {
            for (int64_t i = 0; i < 3; i++) {

    // ---- ./channels.gp:9: ch <- i
    // gp[closures] col 4: captures ch by copy into the lambda: it never changes after the closure
    //     exists and is at most 128 bytes
    // gp[channels] col 4: send: blocks until a receiver is ready or the buffer has room; sending on a
    //     closed channel panics, on a nil channel blocks forever
                gp::send(ch, i);

    // ---- ./channels.gp:10: }
            }

    // ---- ./channels.gp:11: close(ch)
    // gp[channels] col 3: close: wakes the receivers; closing a nil or an already closed channel panics
            gp::close(ch);

    // ---- ./channels.gp:12: }()
        }

    // ---- ./channels.gp:7: go func() {  (continued)
        );

    // ---- ./channels.gp:13: for v := range ch {
    // gp[range] col 2: range over a channel: receives until the channel is closed (gp::range_chan)
        for (auto gp_it0 = gp::range_chan(ch); gp::range_next(gp_it0);) {
            int64_t v = gp_it0.gp_v();

    // ---- ./channels.gp:14: fmt.Println(v)
    // gp[interfaces] col 3: fmt.Println: the runtime's gp::fmt functions take each operand with the
    //     descriptor of its Go type (gp::fmt::as), not boxed into an interface first (an operand of a
    //     non-pointer type with an Error method is, see the allocation note)
            gp::fmt::Println(v);

    // ---- ./channels.gp:15: }
        }

    // ---- ./channels.gp:16: v, ok := <-ch
    // gp[channels] col 11: receive with ok: like a receive, and ok is false once the channel is closed
    //     and empty
        auto gp_t0 = gp::recv2(ch);
        int64_t v = std::get<0>(gp_t0);
        bool ok = std::get<1>(gp_t0);

    // ---- ./channels.gp:17: fmt.Println(v, ok)
        gp::fmt::Println(v, ok);

    // ---- ./channels.gp:18: }
    }

    } // namespace gpkg_main
